```

Three under `allow`, against the `allow` plan: the face's own subject also
selected by `tcp`, so every session over TCP gets `ground`'s grants; and
`ground` narrowed to its face and `radio-b` off `udp`, so each one's
session over a link protocol it is not kept off matches no subject, and is
allowed everything. `narrowed_too_far` reads every face at once, so it
names no face. Exit 1.

```console
$ zenctl check face tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router-allow-narrowed.json5 --face-policy ../examples/zk2/link/radio.face.toml --enrollment ../examples/zk2/link/radio.enrollment.toml --contracts ../examples/zk2/link/radio.v1.toml --default-permission allow --format table
? 1
under default_permission allow, zenoh evaluates no allow rule, so each grant is compiled into denies of its complement, from the contracts given (regenerate on every revision). Deny works by inclusion: a put on a wildcard key is in none and reaches subscribers (R6 discards it), and a wildcard call is in none either, so an operation that allows fan-out executes on every provider for a principal never granted it, only its answers denied (O2 refuses the others). A session matching no subject has no policy and is allowed everything, so authentication must refuse it (§11.2, §11.3)
tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router-allow-narrowed.json5: face radio (far ground): 20 deny rule(s) across subject(s) ground, face:radio; 2 downsampling rule(s) on its transports
  ✗ narrowed_too_far          face -      ground
  ✗ narrowed_too_far          face -      radio-b
  ✗ face_subject_beyond_face  face radio  face:radio
against the plan: 3 difference(s)
  ✗ subject_differs             radio-b     planned cns [] usernames ["radio-b"] link_protocols ["tcp", "udp", "tls", "quic", "serial", "unixpipe", "vsock", "ws"]; configured cns [] usernames ["radio-b"] link_protocols ["tcp", "tls", "quic", "serial", "unixpipe", "vsock", "ws"]
  ✗ subject_differs             face:radio  planned cns [] usernames [] link_protocols ["unixsock-stream"]; configured cns [] usernames [] link_protocols ["unixsock-stream", "tcp"]
  ✗ subject_unplanned_property  ground      configured link_protocols: ["unixsock-stream"]
FAIL
narrowed_too_far ground: under allow, a session presenting this name alone, over a link protocol it is not kept off, matches no subject, so it is allowed everything there: no subject naming no interface and no zid selects that link protocol, among those naming it in the list of its kind and nothing in the other, or no user name and no CN (§2.4, §2.15)
narrowed_too_far radio-b: under allow, a session presenting this name alone, over a link protocol it is not kept off, matches no subject, so it is allowed everything there: no subject naming no interface and no zid selects that link protocol, among those naming it in the list of its kind and nothing in the other, or no user name and no CN (§2.4, §2.15)
face_subject_beyond_face [face radio] face:radio: a subject bound by no identity that a session over the face matches, and that selects a link protocol outside the face's, or every one: sessions off the face match it too, and get what its policies grant beside their own (§2.4, §2.15)
judged against the config file tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router-allow-narrowed.json5 through zenoh's own loader: what a router runs is not observable on the bus, so a file that differs from what it was started with is invisible here (core §11.3, link.v1 §2.14)
tombstone windows not asked: pass --window ADDRESS=SECS for each listed owner of state (link.v1 §2.11)

```

The generator's block with its `enabled` line taken out. zenoh reads an
absent `enabled` as false, and enforces none of the block: every session
over the face gets everything, and writes an owner's keys (`link.v1`
scenarios §12). Since 0.7 (F-124) that is §2.15's own `block_disabled`,
read as zenoh reads it, and not only the comparison's `disabled`. It is
reported beside the block's other findings, here none. Exit 1.

```console
$ zenctl check face tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router-disabled.json5 --face-policy ../examples/zk2/link/radio.face.toml --enrollment ../examples/zk2/link/radio.enrollment.toml --contracts ../examples/zk2/link/radio.v1.toml --format table
? 1
tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router-disabled.json5: face radio (far ground): 8 deny rule(s) across subject(s) ground; 2 downsampling rule(s) on its transports
  ✗ block_disabled  face radio  enabled
against the plan: 1 difference(s)
  ✗ disabled  access_control  planned enabled: true; configured enabled: false
FAIL
block_disabled [face radio] enabled: the block's access control is not enabled, `enabled` false or absent, which zenoh reads as false: the router enforces none of its rules, under either posture, so everything crosses the face; its other findings read the block as written (§2.15)
judged against the config file tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router-disabled.json5 through zenoh's own loader: what a router runs is not observable on the bus, so a file that differs from what it was started with is invisible here (core §11.3, link.v1 §2.14)
tombstone windows not asked: pass --window ADDRESS=SECS for each listed owner of state (link.v1 §2.11)

```

Given the windows the deployment configures (§2.11), every listed owner of
state is checked against each face's outage, a window not given being the
core's 60 s. A window of 2 s below the radio face's 6 s outage is a
finding, though the block itself is clean.

```console
$ zenctl check face tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router.json5 --face-policy ../examples/zk2/link/radio.face.toml --enrollment ../examples/zk2/link/radio.enrollment.toml --contracts ../examples/zk2/link/radio.v1.toml --window lab/radio-a=2 --format ndjson
? 1
{"against":"tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router.json5","comparison":{"answer":"not_established","reason":"tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router.json5 carries the plan whole: 26 rule(s), 5 subject(s), 5 polic(y/ies), enabled, default deny, 1 downsampling item(s)"},"judgement":{"answer":"established"},"namespace":"","notes":[{"text":"window_under_outage [face radio] lab/radio-a: a listed owner of state's tombstone window is below the face's outage: a delete made during a longer outage is lost across the face (§2.11)"},{"cite":"core §11.3, link.v1 §2.14","text":"judged against the config file tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router.json5 through zenoh's own loader: what a router runs is not observable on the bus, so a file that differs from what it was started with is invisible here"},{"text":"the windows are the ones given: whether each owner runs with its window is its configuration, not on the bus (link.v1 §2.11, §5)"}],"report":"face-check"}
{"denies":8,"face":"radio","far":"ground","row":"face","rules":2,"subjects":["ground"]}
{"at":"lab/radio-a","code":"window_under_outage","face":"radio","row":"finding","severity":"error"}
{"face":"radio","given":true,"min_s":6,"row":"window","service":"lab/radio-a","window_s":2}
window_under_outage [face radio] lab/radio-a: a listed owner of state's tombstone window is below the face's outage: a delete made during a longer outage is lost across the face (§2.11)
judged against the config file tests/cmd/fixtures/link/router.json5 through zenoh's own loader: what a router runs is not observable on the bus, so a file that differs from what it was started with is invisible here (core §11.3, link.v1 §2.14)
the windows are the ones given: whether each owner runs with its window is its configuration, not on the bus (link.v1 §2.11, §5)

```

A file that is not there, and a window that is not `ADDRESS=SECS`, are
questions that could not be asked: exit 2, never a 1 claiming the router
guards its faces wrongly.

```console
$ zenctl check face tests/cmd/fixtures/link/no-such-router.json5 --face-policy ../examples/zk2/link/radio.face.toml --enrollment ../examples/zk2/link/radio.enrollment.toml --contracts ../examples/zk2/link/radio.v1.toml
